---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import { readManifest } from "../lib/releases.mjs";
const release = await readManifest();
---

<Site title="A home for your workflows">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow dot">
        {release
          ? `Public beta · ${release.version}`
          : "Visual workflow builder · Public beta coming soon"}
      </p>
      <h1>
        Your workflows.
        <br />
        <em>Every step, in view.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Connect Docker containers, server groups, AI agents, and human
        approvals. Build visually on your machine, then share the workflow with
        your team.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a
          class="button"
          href={release ? "/download/" : "/docs/workflow-builder/"}
        >
          {release ? "Download Runstead" : "Explore the workflow builder"}{" "}
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/docs/">
          Explore the docs
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="builder-demo" aria-labelledby="builder-example-title">
      <div class="canvas-header">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">An example workflow</span>
          <h2 id="builder-example-title">
            From a container to a reviewed rollout.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <span class="canvas-label">Build it step by step</span>
      </div>
      <ol class="flow-steps">
        <li>
          <span class="step-kind">Docker</span>
          <h3>Run checks</h3>
          <p>
            Choose an image.
            <br />
            Set the command.
          </p>
          <span class="step-detail">01 · Container task</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-kind">AI agent</span>
          <h3>Review the output</h3>
          <p>
            Give an agent the context
            <br />
            and a clear task.
          </p>
          <span class="step-detail">02 · Agent task</span>
        </li>
        <li class="human-step">
          <span class="step-kind">Human approval</span>
          <h3>Make the call</h3>
          <p>
            Approve, send back,
            <br />
            or reject the result.
          </p>
          <span class="step-detail">03 · Approval gate</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-kind">Server group</span>
          <h3>Roll out remotely</h3>
          <p>
            Run commands over SSH
            <br />
            across your servers.
          </p>
          <span class="step-detail">04 · Remote command</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="canvas-caption">
        Illustrative flow. Configure your own images, agents, credentials, and
        server targets.{" "}
        <a href="/docs/workflow-builder/">See how the builder works →</a>
      </p>
    </section>
    <div class="under-shot">
      <span>Workflow definitions and logs, stored locally.</span>
      <span>Powered by the Dagu workflow engine.</span>
    </div>
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Different tools. One workflow.</p>
          <h2>
            Build the steps.
            <br />
            Connect the work.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Add a step, choose how it runs, and connect what comes next. Configure
          the workflow visually, with YAML available when you need it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="features builder-features">
        <article class="feature">
          <div class="number">01 / Containers</div>
          <h3>Start from a Docker image.</h3>
          <p>
            Choose an image, set the command, and configure the container's
            environment and mounts. Bring private registry credentials when you
            need them.
          </p>
          <a class="release-link" href="/docs/workflow-builder/">
            Build a container task →
          </a>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <div class="number">02 / Remote commands</div>
          <h3>Reach a whole server group.</h3>
          <p>
            Define SSH machines once, arrange them into rollout groups, and
            choose how many run at once and what happens when a machine fails.
          </p>
          <a class="release-link" href="/docs/workflow-builder/">
            Connect your servers →
          </a>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <div class="number">03 / AI agents</div>
          <h3>Give your agent a clear step.</h3>
          <p>
            Use a command-line agent or an API model alongside ordinary tasks.
            Set the prompt, provide context, and inspect the result as part of
            the run.
          </p>
          <a class="release-link" href="/docs/ai/">
            Add an AI task →
          </a>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <div class="number">04 / Human in the loop</div>
          <h3>Leave room for a decision.</h3>
          <p>
            Collect input with a human task or attach an approval gate to a
            step. Review the output, approve it, send it back, or reject it
            before work continues.
          </p>
          <a class="release-link" href="/docs/workflow-builder/">
            Add a human checkpoint →
          </a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Your devices. Your team. Your organization.</p>
        <h2>
          Build once.
          <br />
          Bring your team along.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Move a project between your devices or share it with teammates across
          your organization. Workflow definitions and reusable configuration
          travel together. Each device supplies its own credentials and chooses
          what runs.
        </p>
        <a class="release-link" href="/docs/sharing/">
          Share a workflow with your team →
        </a>
      </div>
      <ul class="detail-list">
        <li>
          <h3>Take the project to another device</h3>
          <p>
            Export a project and import it on another Runstead installation.
            Bring its workflows, agents, server groups, and defaults together.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Give teammates a shared starting point</h3>
          <p>
            Hand over a repeatable workflow instead of a set of instructions.
            Imported schedules start paused, so each person decides when to run
            them.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Connect to a shared Runstead host</h3>
          <p>
            Let team members use MCP or REST clients with separate API keys and
            role permissions. Configure the network access for your environment.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">From building to running</p>
          <h2>
            Make it repeatable.
            <br />
            Keep it understandable.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Run on demand or set a schedule. Follow each step, inspect its output,
          and find what needs attention. Add inputs, retries, and notifications
          as the workflow grows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="showcase">
        <img
          src="/images/workflows.png"
          alt="Development preview of Runstead showing scheduled jobs and recent runs"
          width="1090"
          height="768"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Development preview of the overview screen. The interface may change
          before launch.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
    <section class="section split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Your machine. Your workspace.</p>
        <h2>
          Keep your work
          <br />
          close to home.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Runstead hosts the workflow engine on your machine. Your workflow
          definitions, execution logs, and backups are stored locally. Tasks and
          connected clients can send data to the services you choose.
        </p>
        <a class="release-link" href="/docs/scheduling/">
          Understand background operation →
        </a>
      </div>
      <ul class="detail-list">
        <li>
          <h3>Keep working in the background</h3>
          <p>
            Keep Runstead running while you work. On macOS, closing the window
            leaves it in the menu bar. Scheduled work needs an awake machine
            with your user signed in.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Connect on your terms</h3>
          <p>
            Use scoped API keys for MCP and REST access. Remote access requires
            your own secure network setup.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Start without an account</h3>
          <p>
            The free plan holds one project. A Dagu Cloud account is only needed
            for Pro.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="closing">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Start small. Make it useful.</p>
        <h2>One workflow is a good start.</h2>
      </div>
      <a class="button" href="/docs/getting-started/">
        Read the getting-started guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Site>
